00:09:25.076 --> 00:09:34.940
<v News>多くの研究者が、利根川さんの業績と、挑戦を続けるその姿勢をたたえています。

00:09:35.639 --> 00:09:39.672
<v Host>Time for a short quiz. Listen and try to answer.

00:09:40.272 --> 00:09:48.120
<v Slow>利根川さんは、何の賞を日本人で初めて受賞しましたか。

00:09:48.919 --> 00:09:54.895
<v Host>The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, which he received in nineteen eighty-seven.

00:09:55.495 --> 00:10:03.727
<v Slow>利根川さんは、何の仕組みを遺伝子のレベルで明らかにしましたか。

00:10:04.528 --> 00:10:10.216
<v Host>The mechanism of immunity — how the body creates a huge variety of antibodies.

00:10:10.816 --> 00:10:20.583
<v Slow>マサチューセッツ工科大学に移ったあと、利根川さんは何の研究に挑みましたか。

00:10:21.384 --> 00:10:25.272
<v Host>Research on the brain, especially how memory is stored.

00:10:25.871 --> 00:10:50.015
<v Host>That's all for today. Susumu Tonegawa, Japan's first Nobel laureate in Physiology or Medicine, has died at eighty-six. He uncovered how our genes build an almost endless variety of antibodies, overturned the common wisdom of biology, and later spent his life taking on the mysteries of memory in the brain. Thanks for listening, and see you tomorrow.

00:10:50.615 --> 00:10:53.015
<v News>また明日。
